1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,935
A server is just a computer that other computers depend on.

2
00:00:04,485 --> 00:00:06,426
When it breaks, people notice quickly.

3
00:00:06,976 --> 00:00:10,444
The good news: a handful of parts cause most failures,

4
00:00:10,444 --> 00:00:13,077
and each one fails in a recognisable way.

5
00:00:13,627 --> 00:00:18,534
Today you meet those parts, and watch each one fail, live, in a safe practice setup.

6
00:00:20,584 --> 00:00:22,845
Every server has the same main parts.

7
00:00:23,395 --> 00:00:25,017
The CPU does the work.

8
00:00:25,567 --> 00:00:30,239
Memory, or RAM, is the fast workspace for whatever is running right now.

9
00:00:30,789 --> 00:00:32,858
The disk stores data for the long term.

10
00:00:33,408 --> 00:00:35,733
The network connects the server to everyone else.

11
00:00:36,283 --> 00:00:39,057
Services are the programs that wait for requests.

12
00:00:39,607 --> 00:00:43,425
And logs are the diary where those programs write down what happened.

13
00:00:43,975 --> 00:00:47,922
Learn which part fails in which way, and troubleshooting gets much easier.

14
00:00:49,972 --> 00:00:51,423
First, the CPU.

15
00:00:51,973 --> 00:00:55,237
It runs instructions, billions of times every second.

16
00:00:55,787 --> 00:00:59,968
Each core does one thing at a time, so a busy server shares its cores out.

17
00:01:00,518 --> 00:01:04,294
When one program hogs the CPU, everything else slows down.

18
00:01:04,844 --> 00:01:07,937
The symptom: slow responses, lag, and a high load.

19
00:01:08,487 --> 00:01:11,879
The first check: which process is using the most CPU?

20
00:01:13,929 --> 00:01:16,254
Let us watch a CPU problem happen.

21
00:01:16,804 --> 00:01:19,556
First, how many cores? Type: nproc

22
00:01:19,686 --> 00:01:21,436
Two cores. That is our budget.

23
00:01:21,986 --> 00:01:28,108
Now start a program that never rests. Type: yes > /dev/null &

24
00:01:28,238 --> 00:01:32,356
The ampersand runs it in the background, and the shell gives it a job number.

25
00:01:32,906 --> 00:01:43,231
Now ask who is busiest. Type: ps -u student -o pid,pcpu,comm --sort=-pcpu

26
00:01:43,361 --> 00:01:47,222
There it is: yes, using about ninety-nine percent of one core.

27
00:01:47,772 --> 00:01:51,804
That is a runaway process. Stop it. Type: kill %1

28
00:01:51,934 --> 00:01:55,198
Now ask again. Press the up arrow twice, then Enter.

29
00:01:58,748 --> 00:01:59,088
Gone.

30
00:01:59,088 --> 00:02:00,584
The CPU is free again.

31
00:02:00,584 --> 00:02:03,168
The pattern: find the busiest process,

32
00:02:03,168 --> 00:02:05,276
then decide whether to stop it.

33
00:02:07,326 --> 00:02:08,350
Next, memory.

34
00:02:08,900 --> 00:02:13,743
Memory holds the data of every running program, and it is much faster than the disk.

35
00:02:14,293 --> 00:02:15,358
But it is limited.

36
00:02:15,358 --> 00:02:16,365
When it runs out,

37
00:02:16,365 --> 00:02:18,437
the system starts swapping to disk,

38
00:02:18,437 --> 00:02:19,562
which is very slow.

39
00:02:20,112 --> 00:02:21,698
If it runs out completely,

40
00:02:21,698 --> 00:02:24,015
the system kills a program to survive.

41
00:02:24,015 --> 00:02:26,149
That event is called out of memory.

42
00:02:26,699 --> 00:02:30,774
The symptom: sudden slowness, or a program that vanishes without warning.

43
00:02:31,324 --> 00:02:33,180
The first check: free dash h.

44
00:02:35,230 --> 00:02:38,131
Let us look at memory. Type: free -h

45
00:02:38,261 --> 00:02:42,485
Total is what exists. Available is what a new program can really use.

46
00:02:43,035 --> 00:02:47,686
Now a greedy program. Look at it first. Type: cat hog.py

47
00:02:47,816 --> 00:02:50,376
It asks for five hundred megabytes in one go.

48
00:02:50,926 --> 00:02:53,928
To copy a small server,

49
00:02:53,928 --> 00:02:59,279
we cap this run at two hundred megabytes.

50
00:02:59,279 --> 00:03:06,457
Type: (ulimit -v 200000; python3 hog.py) 2>&1 | tail -1

51
00:03:06,587 --> 00:03:09,829
Memory error. The program asked for more than it was allowed.

52
00:03:10,379 --> 00:03:12,033
On a real server with no cap,

53
00:03:12,033 --> 00:03:13,915
the kernel would kill it instead,

54
00:03:13,915 --> 00:03:15,969
and the log would say out of memory.

55
00:03:18,019 --> 00:03:19,022
Now the disk.

56
00:03:19,572 --> 00:03:22,409
The disk keeps your files, databases, and logs.

57
00:03:22,959 --> 00:03:27,418
A full disk is one of the most common outages, and one of the easiest to miss.

58
00:03:27,968 --> 00:03:32,597
When there is no room, databases stop writing, logs stop, and programs crash.

59
00:03:33,147 --> 00:03:35,515
The symptom: no space left on device.

60
00:03:36,065 --> 00:03:40,865
The first checks: d f to see which disk is full, then d u to see what is filling it.

61
00:03:42,915 --> 00:03:47,608
Let us fill a disk, safely. We use a small practice disk of only ten megabytes.

62
00:03:48,158 --> 00:03:50,078
Type: df -h data

63
00:03:50,208 --> 00:03:52,790
Size ten megabytes, almost nothing used.

64
00:03:53,340 --> 00:04:05,244
Now write twenty megabytes into it. Type: dd if=/dev/zero of=data/big.bin bs=1M count=20

65
00:04:05,374 --> 00:04:08,083
No space left on device. The disk is full.

66
00:04:08,633 --> 00:04:11,577
Check again. Type: df -h data

67
00:04:11,707 --> 00:04:13,200
One hundred percent used.

68
00:04:13,750 --> 00:04:17,740
Who is using it? Type: du -sh data/*

69
00:04:17,870 --> 00:04:21,496
One file, big dot bin, ten megabytes. Found the culprit.

70
00:04:22,046 --> 00:04:25,609
Remove it. Type: rm data/big.bin

71
00:04:25,739 --> 00:04:28,939
One last check. Type: df -h data

72
00:04:29,069 --> 00:04:33,634
Back to zero. Check disk space early, before a full disk stops your service.

73
00:04:35,684 --> 00:04:39,801
Now services: the programs that answer requests, like a web server.

74
00:04:40,351 --> 00:04:43,594
A service must be running, and it must be listening on a port.

75
00:04:44,144 --> 00:04:48,816
A port is a numbered door on the server. Only one program can hold each door.

76
00:04:49,366 --> 00:04:50,841
Clients get codes back.

77
00:04:50,841 --> 00:04:52,509
Two hundred means success.

78
00:04:52,509 --> 00:04:55,396
Four hundred four means that page is missing.

79
00:04:55,396 --> 00:04:58,603
Five hundred range means the server itself failed.

80
00:04:59,153 --> 00:05:01,065
The symptom: connection refused,

81
00:05:01,065 --> 00:05:02,080
or an error code.

82
00:05:02,080 --> 00:05:03,932
The first check: is it running,

83
00:05:03,932 --> 00:05:05,127
and is it listening?

84
00:05:07,177 --> 00:05:10,057
Let us run a tiny web server that comes with Python.

85
00:05:10,607 --> 00:05:20,548
Type: python3 -m http.server 8080 >server.log 2>&1 &

86
00:05:20,678 --> 00:05:29,745
Is it reachable? Ask for its headline. Type: curl -sI localhost:8080 | head -1

87
00:05:29,875 --> 00:05:32,222
Two hundred, O K. The service is up.

88
00:05:32,772 --> 00:05:42,777
Now ask for a page that does not exist. Type: curl -sI localhost:8080/nope | head -1

89
00:05:42,907 --> 00:05:47,984
Four hundred four. The server is fine; that page is missing. A different problem.

90
00:05:48,534 --> 00:06:00,588
Now start a second copy on the same port. Type: python3 -m http.server 8080 2>&1 | tail -1

91
00:06:00,718 --> 00:06:04,259
Address already in use. Only one program can hold a port.

92
00:06:04,809 --> 00:06:11,081
Who is listening? Type: ss -ltn sport = :8080

93
00:06:11,211 --> 00:06:17,419
The state is listen, on port eight zero eight zero. Now stop the server. Type: kill %1

94
00:06:17,549 --> 00:06:23,416
Ask again. Type: curl -sS localhost:8080

95
00:06:23,546 --> 00:06:24,650
Failed to connect.

96
00:06:24,650 --> 00:06:25,939
Nothing is listening,

97
00:06:25,939 --> 00:06:27,350
so the service is down.

98
00:06:27,350 --> 00:06:29,988
That is what connection refused looks like.

99
00:06:32,038 --> 00:06:33,211
Last, the logs.

100
00:06:33,761 --> 00:06:38,412
When a service fails, the screen often shows nothing. The reason is in its log.

101
00:06:38,962 --> 00:06:43,399
Logs record each request, each error, each start and stop, with a time.

102
00:06:43,949 --> 00:06:47,590
Two tools do most of the work: tail shows the end of a file,

103
00:06:47,590 --> 00:06:49,411
and grep finds matching lines.

104
00:06:49,961 --> 00:06:52,457
Rule: do not guess. Read the log first.

105
00:06:54,507 --> 00:06:58,603
This log came from a real run of the same web server. Let us read it.

106
00:06:59,153 --> 00:07:03,590
Show the last lines. Type: tail -n 4 server.log

107
00:07:03,720 --> 00:07:07,688
Each line has the client, the time, the request, and the result code.

108
00:07:08,238 --> 00:07:13,102
Now find only the problems. Type: grep 404 server.log

109
00:07:13,232 --> 00:07:16,752
These are the failed requests, with the reason: file not found.

110
00:07:17,302 --> 00:07:21,441
Count them. Type: grep -c 404 server.log

111
00:07:21,571 --> 00:07:22,990
Two lines: the request,

112
00:07:22,990 --> 00:07:24,533
and the reason it failed.

113
00:07:24,533 --> 00:07:27,864
That is how a log turns a vague complaint into a fact.

114
00:07:29,914 --> 00:07:32,239
Put it together as one routine, in order.

115
00:07:32,789 --> 00:07:34,453
One: is the service running?

116
00:07:35,003 --> 00:07:37,222
Two: is it reachable, on the right port?

117
00:07:37,772 --> 00:07:40,887
Three: are the CPU, memory, and disk exhausted?

118
00:07:41,437 --> 00:07:43,015
Four: what do the logs say?

119
00:07:43,565 --> 00:07:46,168
Work in that order, and write down what you find.

120
00:07:48,218 --> 00:07:49,818
Match the symptom to the part.

121
00:07:50,368 --> 00:07:54,805
Everything is slow: CPU or memory. Check with p s and free.

122
00:07:55,355 --> 00:07:58,257
A program vanished: out of memory. Check the logs.

123
00:07:58,807 --> 00:08:02,817
No space left on device: disk. Check with d f and d u.

124
00:08:03,367 --> 00:08:08,701
Connection refused: the service is down or not listening. Check with s s and curl.

125
00:08:09,251 --> 00:08:13,155
Four oh four: the service is fine. The page or path is wrong.

126
00:08:15,205 --> 00:08:17,061
Watch out for four common myths.

127
00:08:17,611 --> 00:08:22,646
Myth one: slow means the network. Often it is the CPU, memory, or disk.

128
00:08:23,196 --> 00:08:28,422
Myth two: a full disk only means no new files. It can stop a database completely.

129
00:08:28,972 --> 00:08:33,666
Myth three: four oh four means the server is down. It means the server answered.

130
00:08:34,216 --> 00:08:38,632
Myth four: restart first, look later. A restart can erase the evidence.

131
00:08:39,182 --> 00:08:40,270
Check, then act.

132
00:08:42,320 --> 00:08:44,645
Here is how you check your own understanding.

133
00:08:45,195 --> 00:08:49,675
Name the six parts: CPU, memory, disk, network, services, logs.

134
00:08:50,225 --> 00:08:53,297
Find the busiest process, and stop a runaway one.

135
00:08:53,847 --> 00:08:56,471
Read memory with free, and explain available.

136
00:08:57,021 --> 00:08:58,963
Find a full disk, and what fills it.

137
00:08:59,513 --> 00:09:03,289
Tell down from missing: connection refused versus four oh four.

138
00:09:03,839 --> 00:09:06,249
Find a failed request in a log with grep.

139
00:09:06,799 --> 00:09:08,421
To pass, you need all six.

140
00:09:10,471 --> 00:09:12,754
Your turn, in the virtual machine you built.

141
00:09:13,304 --> 00:09:17,016
One: run nproc and free dash h, and write down your numbers.

142
00:09:17,566 --> 00:09:20,958
Two: start a yes process, find it with p s, and kill it.

143
00:09:21,508 --> 00:09:25,817
Three: run d f on your system, and find your biggest folder with d u.

144
00:09:26,367 --> 00:09:28,404
Four: start the Python web server,

145
00:09:28,404 --> 00:09:30,620
get a two hundred and a four oh four,

146
00:09:30,620 --> 00:09:32,596
then stop it and see the refusal.

147
00:09:33,146 --> 00:09:35,941
Five: grep your own server log for four oh four.

148
00:09:36,491 --> 00:09:39,798
Mark yourself on the rubric. Pause the video, and do it now.

149
00:09:55,348 --> 00:09:56,223
Quick check.

150
00:09:56,773 --> 00:09:58,565
Name the six parts of a server.

151
00:09:59,115 --> 00:10:01,355
Which command finds the busiest process?

152
00:10:01,905 --> 00:10:04,209
What does no space left on device point to?

153
00:10:04,759 --> 00:10:08,279
What is the difference between connection refused and four oh four?

154
00:10:08,829 --> 00:10:13,394
Objective covered: MC02-O07.

155
00:10:13,944 --> 00:10:16,013
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

156
00:10:26,563 --> 00:10:28,953
Next lesson: tracing one web request.

157
00:10:29,503 --> 00:10:31,380
Subscribe, so you do not miss it.
